WHAT THE USER SAYS NOW, AFTER USING IT
The detent does not work as drawn. Computed on this version, riding over a notch takes about 202 N.
The curved wall that carries the bump reaches 7.2% strain at its root, where PLA allows 1.38% for
something that bends again and again, and 6.1% between layers, where it allows 0.69%. Turned by
hand, the crank would not click: it would stall, or the wall would break.

Keep the gears, the pins, the caps and the crank as they are. Give the detent an arm that clicks
with the force of a hand on the crank, between 2 and 10 N at the bump, under the limits of PLA for
repeated use, still printed in place. Tell me the force and the strain it ends up with, what to
print first to try the click, and which parameter to move if it turns out too stiff or too soft.

What the user reports is a fact about the real part, and it outranks every number that said otherwise: find which assumption it breaks, write the brief again with that (design_brief), and change what it asks for. A number from G6 that said this could not happen says the model was wrong.

